// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  All-in-One ULTIMATE PRO — EMA + Tunnel/Vegas + Trend Lines + MSB-OB + SMC
//                            + Confidence Meter + MACD/Volume Pane
//  Trend Lines © LonesomeTheBlue | MSB-OB © EmreKb (MPL 2.0)
//  Smart Money Concepts © LuxAlgo (CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)
//  Double Tunnel © Gilbert0967 (periods now Sykes' 144/169 + 576/676) | Vegas EMA200 © rsu9111 (MPL 2.0)
//  NOTE: SMC portion is non-commercial per LuxAlgo's license. Keep attribution.
//  Panels: Confidence Meter = top-right · MTF Bias = middle-right (defaults)
//          ⑭ MACD + Volume = separate pane below the price chart
//  ⑨ Reactive S/R Zones — ORIGINAL engine (pivot clustering, volume-weighted
//     anchors, touch-scored strength, price-side polarity, decay): your own code.
//  ⑬ Multi-Timeframe Bias — 15m/30m/1H long-short read (default OFF).
//
//  ★ CLEAN CHART MODE (① Modules, default ON) — one switch that hides the
//    visual noise so the S/R zones and trend are readable: EMA 50/100,
//    Tunnel 2, SMC structure lines, order blocks, and FVG. Surplus trendlines
//    are DIMMED, not deleted. CALCULATIONS ARE UNCHANGED — the Confidence
//    Meter score is identical with it on or off (this is now true; see v2 note 2).
//
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  FORKED FROM All-in-One_ULTIMATE.pine — WHAT CHANGED IN PRO
//
//  1. VOLUME PROFILE REMOVED. It was a fully self-contained module (nothing
//     outside it read a vp*-prefixed identifier) — deleted wholesale rather
//     than disabled, so it costs nothing and can't be half-left-on by mistake.
//     The Confidence Meter's own Volume factor never depended on it (it always
//     used a rolling volume-weighted mean, computed per-bar so it can't repaint
//     — the drawn profile only ever existed on the last bar).
//
//  2. CONFIDENCE PANEL + MTF BIAS PANEL redesigned for scanability, not just
//     accuracy. Both now: tint each row's background (not just its text) by
//     that row's own bull/bear/neutral reading, so the panel reads as a color
//     shape before you read a single word; carry a stronger, higher-contrast
//     header bar tinted by the overall bias; and separate the factor breakdown
//     behind its own labeled divider instead of running straight into the core
//     rows. The MTF panel's Support/Resistance column now colors R and S
//     differently (R leans warm/ceiling, S leans cool/floor) instead of both
//     printing in the same flat gray. Every underlying number and threshold is
//     unchanged — this is a presentation pass, not a scoring change.
//
//  3. DOUBLE TUNNEL FIXED + NOW ACTUALLY DOUBLE. Tunnel 2 (the outer band)
//     was gated by `not cleanMode`, and Clean Chart Mode defaults ON — so out
//     of the box only Tunnel 1 ever drew, and "Double Tunnel" was a single
//     line with no way to tell from looking at the chart. Tunnel 2 is now
//     always-on core drawing whenever "Show Double Tunnel" is on, same as
//     Tunnel 1. Its default periods also changed from Gilbert0967's generic
//     2× convention (288/338) to Sykes' own 4× big-tunnel periods (576/676) —
//     matching Sykes_Vegas_Tunnel_Pro.pine's EMA576/676 exactly, since that
//     is the tunnel this fork is named for. This DOES change the Confidence
//     Meter's Tunnel factor (weight 10) and the MTF scorer's tunnel factor
//     (weight 15) versus the untouched original file, unlike items 1-2 above
//     — a deliberate, requested change, not a side effect.
//
//  4. ⑭ MACD + VOLUME PANE ADDED. The source file had no MACD anywhere and
//     no dedicated volume plot — only a silent per-bar volume-weighted mean
//     feeding the Confidence Meter. Getting a REAL separate pane (not squares
//     crammed onto the price scale) required flipping the whole script from
//     `overlay=true` to `overlay=false` and adding `force_overlay=true` to
//     every one of the ~57 pre-existing plot/fill/hline/bgcolor/label/line/
//     box/table calls so they stay pinned to the price chart exactly as
//     before — only the new MACD+Volume calls were left unforced, so only
//     they land in the new pane. Verified mechanically (every pre-existing
//     drawing call now carries the flag, the 6 new ones don't) rather than by
//     eye, since a single missed call would have silently misplaced a line,
//     box, or SMC zone into the wrong pane. Same asset-agnostic volume
//     normalization as Sykes_Vegas_Tunnel_Pro.pine's v2 fix — see its section
//     13 for the full reasoning.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  WHAT CHANGED IN v3 — the abstain rule, finished
//
//  v2 introduced the right idea and applied it to 2 of the 7 score factors.
//  v3 finishes it and fixes two unrelated defects found on the way.
//
//  1. FIVE OF THE SEVEN FACTORS COULD NOT ABSTAIN. A factor with no reading
//     voted a confident "neutral 0" while keeping its full weight in the
//     denominator. Two ways that bit:
//       · SMC holds the LARGEST weight (25). fSMC reads internalTrend.bias,
//         which is only written inside `if showSMC`. Switch the SMC module off
//         and the bias holds its initial 0 for the entire run — a quarter of
//         the meter reporting a neutral read on a module that never ran.
//       · EMA stack, price-vs-200, Vegas and Tunnel are na until their longest
//         average fills — 338 bars for the outer tunnel. `close > na` and
//         `close < na` are BOTH false, so each scored 0. On a fresh chart or a
//         new listing that is 60 of 100 points voting on data nobody had, and
//         the score sat near 50 looking like a real balanced tape.
//     All seven can now abstain, and the panel's new BASIS row prints the
//     weight actually in play instead of implying 100.
//
//  2. THE MTF PANEL HAD THE SAME HOLE, WHERE IT MATTERS MORE. A 1H row needs
//     204 hourly bars before Vegas exists; TradingView serves a limited number
//     of higher-timeframe bars on a low chart timeframe. A row could print a
//     confident "Neutral 50" assembled almost entirely from missing inputs.
//     Unreadable rows now return na and the panel says "no data" — which also
//     stops three blank rows from reading as a genuine mixed market in Align.
//
//  3. THE WRONG ORDER BLOCK WAS BEING DELETED. The legacy MSB zone sweep called
//     array.shift(), which removes index 0 — the OLDEST box — regardless of
//     which one price had actually mitigated. Break the newest of three zones
//     and it deleted the oldest, leaving the mitigated zone on the chart still
//     extending itself as if untouched. It also mutated the array while a
//     `for … in` walked it, so one zone per deletion was skipped unchecked.
//
//  4. CLEAN CHART MODE SILENTLY KILLED THE ORDER-BLOCK ALERTS. deleteOrderBlocks()
//     draws nothing — it retires mitigated blocks and raises the four OB
//     breakout alerts — but it was gated on cleanMode, which is ON by default.
//     Bookkeeping now follows the module input; only drawing follows cleanMode.
//     (FVG is detected by the same call that draws it, so its two alerts still
//     require Clean Mode off. That is now stated in the tooltip rather than
//     left to be discovered.)
//
//  Unchanged: none of this is backtested. The meter is a confluence read, not
//  an edge, and a higher number is not a better trade.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//  WHAT CHANGED IN v2 — measurement fixes, not cosmetics
//  Every item below is a case where the indicator reported something that was
//  not true. Behaviour changes are called out so nothing surprises you.
//
//  1. MTF BIAS PANEL REPAINTED, and the code claimed it didn't.
//     request.security(..., lookahead_off) returns the CURRENTLY FORMING
//     higher-timeframe bar in realtime. So the 1H row could read LONG, you act
//     on it, the 1H candle closes bearish — and the history then shows a bar
//     where that LONG never existed. The panel now reads the last CLOSED HTF
//     bar (expr[1] + lookahead_on), which is identical on history and in
//     realtime. VISIBLE CHANGE: rows update when each HTF bar closes, not tick
//     by tick. That lag is real information, not a downgrade.
//
//  2. CLEAN CHART MODE SILENTLY CHANGED THE SCORE. It capped the trendline
//     engine to 2 lines per side, so lines that would have been broken were
//     already retired and their break never fired — moving the Confidence
//     Meter's TL factor and the score itself. Clean Mode now caps DRAWING
//     only; the engine always runs at your settings.
//
//  3. THE TUNNEL FACTOR WAS BEARISH-BIASED. Bull required a close above
//     dt_ma1 — the tunnel's UPPER line in an uptrend. Bear required a close
//     below dt_ma2, which in a downtrend is ALSO the upper line. So a close
//     sitting INSIDE the tunnel scored a full -1 while the mirror-image bull
//     case scored 0. Both sides now require price fully outside both tunnels.
//
//  4. THE SCORE COULD NOT REACH 0 OR 100. The trendline factor holds 5 of the
//     100 weight points but only spoke on the exact bar of a break — roughly
//     one bar in a thousand. The other 999 it contributed 0 while still
//     sitting in the denominator, so 95 and 100 meant the same thing. A break
//     now stays on the books for a memory window, and when a factor has NO
//     OPINION (no recent break; volume window not yet full) its weight is
//     removed from the denominator instead of being counted as a neutral vote.
//
//  5. S/R ZONES REGISTERED PHANTOM BREAKS. "Which side was price on last bar"
//     was measured against the CURRENT anchor — but the anchor moves when a
//     new swing is absorbed. A re-anchor could flip the recorded side and log
//     a break that never happened; two of those retire the zone. Now compared
//     against the anchor as it stood on the previous bar.
//
//  6. S/R ZONES DECAYED WHILE PRICE SAT INSIDE THEM. A bar closing inside a
//     zone was neither a touch nor a break, so the zone's activity clock kept
//     running — it could expire at the exact moment it mattered most.
//
//  7. A FRESH, UNTESTED PIVOT COULD EVICT YOUR STRONGEST LEVEL. At capacity
//     the weakest zone was deleted unconditionally to make room for a new one
//     of strength 2 — the opposite of "only the strongest N are kept". The
//     newcomer must now out-rank what it replaces (strength discounted by
//     distance from price, so stale far-away levels still lose).
//
//  8. Smaller: S/R merged into the FIRST zone in range rather than the
//     NEAREST; the MTF panel hard-coded 20/50/100/200/144/288 and ignored your
//     EMA and Tunnel inputs while claiming to be "scored the SAME way"; the
//     High-Confidence alerts ignored "Confirm Signals on Bar Close" and fired
//     intrabar; the signal layer's permanent direction lock meant a second
//     LONG could never fire until a SHORT had (valid setups vanished because
//     of unrelated history) — now an explicit bar cooldown, default 0; the
//     Volume Profile's "Structure: Buyers dominant" was a POC-position claim
//     dressed up as order flow; the Regime row implied signals were blocked
//     even with the ADX filter switched off.
// ════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
//@version=6
// max_bars_back was 5000, which is what made this script expensive: Pine
// allocates that history buffer for EVERY series, and this file has dozens.
// It was set to match the 5000 MAXVAL of three inputs whose DEFAULTS are 360
// and 600 — paying for a ceiling nobody used. 1500 covers the longest real
// lookback (the 676-period outer tunnel) with room to spare, and those three
// inputs are now capped to fit inside it rather than promising a range the
// runtime cannot honour.
// Object caps sized to what the engines can ACTUALLY draw, not to the maximum
// Pine allows. Worst case with every input at its own maximum:
//     lines  — 10 active × 2 sides + 30 broken = 50, plus SMC structure
//     boxes  — 12 S/R zones + SMC order blocks + FVG
//     labels — one per trendline + zone and SMC labels
// 500 of each was ~3× more than reachable, and Pine allocates for the ceiling
// it is given. These still leave large headroom; overflow degrades gracefully
// (Pine drops the oldest object) rather than erroring, so the cost of being
// wrong here is one stale drawing, not a crash.
indicator("All-in-One ULTIMATE Pro", shorttitle="AIO Pro", overlay=false, max_lines_count=200, max_boxes_count=150, max_labels_count=150, max_bars_back=1500)

// ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
// ║  ① MASTER MODULE TOGGLES                               ║
// ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
gM = "① Modules — master on/off"
showEMA   = input.bool(true,  "EMAs",                group=gM, tooltip="Plot the 4 moving averages")
showTL    = input.bool(true,  "Trend Lines",         group=gM, tooltip="Auto-drawn trendlines from pivots")
showMSB   = input.bool(false, "MSB + Order Blocks (legacy)", group=gM, tooltip="EmreKb market-structure + OB/BB zones. OFF by default — overlaps with SMC below.")
showSMC   = input.bool(true,  "Smart Money Concepts", group=gM, tooltip="LuxAlgo SMC: structure, order blocks, FVG, EQH/EQL, zones")
showSIG   = input.bool(false, "Signal Layer (Long/Short)", group=gM, tooltip="Confluence signal. OFF by default — not trading advice.")
showPanel = input.bool(true,  "Confidence Panel",    group=gM, tooltip="Top-right live status table")
hideText  = input.bool(true,  "Hide All Text Labels", group=gM, tooltip="Keep blocks and lines, but remove all words (MSB, EQH/EQL, Premium, etc.) from the chart. Info panels are unaffected.")
cleanMode = input.bool(true,  "★ CLEAN CHART MODE", group=gM, tooltip="ONE switch that removes the visual noise so the S/R zones and trend are actually readable. This indicator can otherwise draw 9 moving averages, 14 trendlines, SMC structure lines and order blocks at once.\n\nWhen ON it hides: EMA 50/100 (keeps 20 + 200), SMC internal structure lines, SMC order blocks, and FVG.\nIt also caps trendlines to 2 active + 2 broken per side.\n\nBoth Vegas tunnels (144/169 and 576/676) are core drawing now, not clutter — Clean Mode no longer hides Tunnel 2.\n\nThe Confidence Meter score is identical either way — every factor it reads is computed regardless of this switch.\n\nONE exception, stated plainly: Fair Value Gaps are DETECTED by the same code that draws them, so while Clean Mode is on the two FVG alerts cannot fire. FVG is off by default. Order-block alerts are NOT affected — that was a v3 fix; they used to go silent here too.\n\nTurn OFF to see every layer again.")

// ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
// ║  ② EMA SETTINGS                                        ║
// ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
gE = "② EMA"
ema20len  = input.int(20,  "Fast",   minval=1, group=gE, inline="e1")
ema50len  = input.int(50,  "Medium", minval=1, group=gE, inline="e2")
ema100len = input.int(100, "Slow",   minval=1, group=gE, inline="e3")
ema200len = input.int(200, "Trend",  minval=1, group=gE, inline="e4")
emaFastCol = input.color(color.yellow,                "", group=gE, inline="e1")
emaMedCol  = input.color(color.new(color.orange, 0),  "", group=gE, inline="e2")
emaSlowCol = input.color(color.new(color.fuchsia, 0), "", group=gE, inline="e3")
emaTrendCol= input.color(color.new(color.blue, 0),    "", group=gE, inline="e4")

emaFast = ta.ema(close, ema20len)
emaMed  = ta.ema(close, ema50len)
emaSlow = ta.ema(close, ema100len)
emaTrend= ta.ema(close, ema200len)

plot(showEMA ? emaFast  : na, "EMA Fast",  color=emaFastCol,  linewidth=1, force_overlay=true)
plot(showEMA and not cleanMode ? emaMed  : na, "EMA Medium",color=emaMedCol,   linewidth=1, force_overlay=true)
plot(showEMA and not cleanMode ? emaSlow : na, "EMA Slow",  color=emaSlowCol,  linewidth=1, force_overlay=true)
plot(showEMA ? emaTrend : na, "EMA Trend", color=emaTrendCol, linewidth=2, force_overlay=true)

// ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
// ║  ② b  DOUBLE TUNNEL + VEGAS EMA200 TREND LINE          ║
// ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
gDT = "② Double Tunnel"
showTunnel  = input.bool(true,  "Show Double Tunnel", group=gDT)
showVegas   = input.bool(true,  "Show Vegas EMA200 Trend Line", group=gDT, tooltip="EMA200 smoothed by SMA5 — green when rising, red when falling.")

// ── MA type helper (converts string → value) ──
f_ma(source, length, maType) =>
    switch maType
        "SMA" => ta.sma(source, length)
        "EMA" => ta.ema(source, length)
        "WMA" => ta.wma(source, length)
        "RMA" => ta.rma(source, length)
        => ta.ema(source, length)

// Tunnel 1 (inner): MA1=144, MA2=169
dt_ma1Period = input.int(144, "Tunnel 1 — Period A", minval=1, group=gDT, inline="t1")
dt_ma1Type   = input.string("EMA", "", options=["SMA","EMA","WMA","RMA"], group=gDT, inline="t1")
dt_ma2Period = input.int(169, "/ B", minval=1, group=gDT, inline="t1")
dt_ma2Type   = input.string("EMA", "", options=["SMA","EMA","WMA","RMA"], group=gDT, inline="t1")
// ── tunnel palette, redesigned 2026-08-14 ───────────────────────────────────
// It was orange/red for Tunnel 1 and lime/gray for Tunnel 2: four unrelated
// hues, so the chart read as "tunnel 1 vs tunnel 2" when the thing you
// actually need to see is "bull vs bear". Orange bull against lime bull is
// two different answers to the same question.
//
// Now BOTH tunnels use one hue per direction — green up, rose down — and the
// two tunnels are told apart by INTENSITY instead: the inner 144/169 tunnel
// is the nearer, more actionable band and reads stronger; the outer 576/676
// is context and sits back. Same greens and roses as the web dashboard
// (#2ee88a / #ff4d6d), so the chart and the site agree.
//
// The two greens also differ in LIGHTNESS, not just hue, so the pair still
// separates for the ~8% of men with red-green deficiency.
dt_t1Bull    = input.color(color.new(#2ee88a, 78), "Bull Fill", group=gDT, inline="t1c")
dt_t1Bear    = input.color(color.new(#ff4d6d, 78), "Bear Fill", group=gDT, inline="t1c")

// Tunnel 2 (outer): MA3=576, MA4=676 — Sykes' own big-tunnel periods (4× the
// inner tunnel), not Gilbert0967's generic 2×/288/338 double-tunnel default.
// Matches Sykes_Vegas_Tunnel_Pro.pine's EMA576/676 exactly.
dt_ma3Period = input.int(576, "Tunnel 2 — Period A", minval=1, group=gDT, inline="t2")
dt_ma3Type   = input.string("EMA", "", options=["SMA","EMA","WMA","RMA"], group=gDT, inline="t2")
dt_ma4Period = input.int(676, "/ B", minval=1, group=gDT, inline="t2")
dt_ma4Type   = input.string("EMA", "", options=["SMA","EMA","WMA","RMA"], group=gDT, inline="t2")
dt_t2Bull    = input.color(color.new(#2ee88a, 91), "Bull Fill", group=gDT, inline="t2c")
dt_t2Bear    = input.color(color.new(#ff4d6d, 91), "Bear Fill", group=gDT, inline="t2c")

dt_ma1 = f_ma(close, dt_ma1Period, dt_ma1Type)
dt_ma2 = f_ma(close, dt_ma2Period, dt_ma2Type)
dt_ma3 = f_ma(close, dt_ma3Period, dt_ma3Type)
dt_ma4 = f_ma(close, dt_ma4Period, dt_ma4Type)

// Vegas EMA200: smoothed EMA200, colored by direction
vegasEMA200 = ta.sma(ta.ema(close, 200), 5)
vegasCol    = ta.change(vegasEMA200) > 0 ? color.new(color.green, 0) : color.new(color.red, 0)

// Plots
// PRO: Tunnel 2 used to be hidden by Clean Chart Mode (cleanMode is ON by
// default), so the "double" in Double Tunnel was invisible out of the box —
// only Tunnel 1 ever showed. Both tunnels are now core, always-on drawing
// when showTunnel is on; Clean Mode still governs everything else it always did.
// Each tunnel's EDGES take that tunnel's own direction too, so a band that
// flips reads as a colour change rather than staying orange through both
// states. Inner edges are solid-ish and 2px; outer edges stay thin and faint
// so they frame without competing.
dt_t1Up = dt_ma1 > dt_ma2 and dt_ma1 >= dt_ma1[1]
dt_t2Up = dt_ma3 > dt_ma4 and dt_ma3 >= dt_ma3[1]
dt_t1Edge = color.new(dt_t1Up ? #2ee88a : #ff4d6d, 30)
dt_t2Edge = color.new(dt_t2Up ? #2ee88a : #ff4d6d, 62)

dt_l1 = plot(showTunnel ? dt_ma1 : na, "Tunnel1-A", color=dt_t1Edge, linewidth=2, force_overlay=true)
dt_l2 = plot(showTunnel ? dt_ma2 : na, "Tunnel1-B", color=dt_t1Edge, linewidth=2, force_overlay=true)
dt_l3 = plot(showTunnel ? dt_ma3 : na, "Tunnel2-A", color=dt_t2Edge, linewidth=1, force_overlay=true)
dt_l4 = plot(showTunnel ? dt_ma4 : na, "Tunnel2-B", color=dt_t2Edge, linewidth=1, force_overlay=true)

// fill() has no force_overlay param (CE10120) — it doesn't need one, since it
// fills the region between two plot() outputs and inherits their pane. Both
// dt_l1/dt_l2 and dt_l3/dt_l4 already carry force_overlay=true, so their fill
// follows them onto the main chart automatically.
fill(dt_l1, dt_l2, color=showTunnel ? (dt_t1Up ? dt_t1Bull : dt_t1Bear) : na, title="Tunnel 1 Fill")
fill(dt_l3, dt_l4, color=showTunnel ? (dt_t2Up ? dt_t2Bull : dt_t2Bear) : na, title="Tunnel 2 Fill")

plot(showVegas ? vegasEMA200 : na, "Vegas EMA200", color=vegasCol, linewidth=2, force_overlay=true)

// ╔═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╗
// ║  ③ TREND LINE SETTINGS                                 ║
// ╚═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════╝
gT = "③ Trend Lines"
startyear  = input.int(2020, "Start Year",  group=gT, inline="d1")
startmonth = input.int(1,    "Month",       group=gT, inline="d1")
startday   = input.int(1,    "Day",         group=gT, inline="d1")
prd        = input.int(20, "Pivot Period",  minval=10, maxval=50, group=gT, tooltip="Bars each side to confirm a pivot.")
PPnum      = input.int(3,  "Pivots to Check", minval=2, maxval=6, group=gT)
tlMaxScan  = input.int(600, "Max Validation Scan (bars)", minval=100, maxval=1400, group=gT, tooltip="RE10140 FIX. Confirming a trendline means walking every bar from the anchor pivot to the present to check price never broke it. That walk used to run to bar_index with no upper bound, nested inside two more loops — so its cost grew with chart length until it blew past Pine's 500ms-per-bar limit and the script died with a runtime error.\n\nThe walk is now capped at this many bars. The line's slope and price math are UNCHANGED — the scan simply starts later and its running price is advanced to match, so a line that would have been drawn is still drawn identically. The only difference: a break that happened MORE than this many bars ago no longer invalidates the line.\n\nLower it if you still hit RE10140 on very long charts or low timeframes.")
maxActiveTL = input.int(4, "Max Active Lines (each side)", minval=1, maxval=10, group=gT)
// DEFAULT CHANGED 6 -> 0 (2026-08-14, owner: "if the trend line become useless
// just delete it"). A broken trendline IS the useless one — price has already
// gone through it, so it no longer describes anything the chart is doing, and
// six of them left dashed across the candles is most of the clutter Clean Mode
// was invented to fight. Raise it if you want the history back.
//
// This is a DRAWING change only. The engine still detects every break, so the
// Confidence Meter's trendline factor is untouched — the same distinction v2
// had to make when Clean Mode was capping the engine instead of the drawing.
keepBroken  = input.int(0, "Keep Broken Lines", minval=0, maxval=30, group=gT, tooltip="Dashed history lines kept after a break. 0 (default) = delete the moment price breaks through, because a broken line no longer describes anything.\n\nThe break is still DETECTED either way — the Confidence Meter's trendline factor does not change with this setting.")
touchTolPct = input.float(0.1, "Touch Tolerance %", 0.0, 2.0, 0.01, group=gT, tooltip="How close price must come to count as a touch.")
showTLlabels = input.bool(true, "Show Touch Labels", group=gT)
// RENAMED and defaulted OFF. It was called "Break Alerts" and controlled only
// the × MARKERS — the alertcondition below fires either way. So switching it
// off to stop the alerts did nothing, and leaving it on to keep them was
// really just keeping ×'s. That is the same shape as the v3 defect where Clean
// Mode silently killed the order-block alerts, in reverse: a name that
// promises something the switch does not do.
tlBreakMarks = input.bool(false, "Show break × markers", group=gT, tooltip="× where price closes through a trendline. Off by default — it marks an event that has already passed, and the line itself is deleted on break.\n\nThe 'Trendline Broken' ALERT is independent of this switch and fires either way.")
tlBaseWidth = input.int(1, "Line Width", minval=1, maxval=4, group=gT, tooltip="Base trendline thickness. Lines thicken slightly with each touch, up to width+2.")
utcol      = input.color(color.lime, "Up", inline="tcol", group=gT)
dtcol      = input.color(color.red,  "Down", inline="tcol", group=gT)

cleanKeepTL = input.int(2, "Clean Mode: lines shown per side", minval=1, maxval=10, group=gT, tooltip="While CLEAN CHART MODE is on, only this many active (and this many broken) trendlines per side stay visible. The rest are made transparent.\n\nv2: this is a DRAWING cap. Previously Clean Mode capped the trendline ENGINE, which retired lines early — so a break that would have fired never did, and the Confidence Meter's score quietly differed between Clean Mode on and off. The engine now always runs at your 'Max Active Lines' setting.")

// v2 FIX (note 2): the engine runs at the user's settings in BOTH modes.
maxActiveTL_e = maxActiveTL
keepBroken_e  = keepBroken

